   "text": "I read the molecule as a macrocycle fused to a substituted benzimidazole.\n\n**Macrocycle**\n- A cyanobenzene (nitrile at the top) has two meta substituents: a CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2 chain and a CH2\u2013O link.\n- The CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2 chain runs to the central fluorobenzene.\n- The CH2\u2013O link runs to the 2-position of a pyridine.\n- The pyridine's 6-position is bonded directly to the central fluorobenzene.\n\n**Central fluorobenzene**\n- Its ring carbons carry, in order: the CH2\u2013O chain, the CH2 linking to the benzimidazole, a CH, the fluorine, the pyridine, and a second CH.\n\n**Benzimidazole**\n- C2 carries the CH2 that links to the central benzene.\n- N1 carries a CH2 attached to an oxetan-2-yl group.\n- On the benzo ring, C4 bears OCH2CH2OMe, C6 bears CO2Me, and C5 and C7 are CH.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- The only stereo marking is the hashed bond from the oxetane C2 to the CH2 on N1.\n- The priorities at that carbon are O, then CH2N, then ring CH2, with H lowest.\n- With the CH2N group pointing away and H toward the viewer, the centre works out as S.\n- In the SMILES, `[C@@H]` written as `n1C[C@@H]1CCO1` gives S.\n\nSMILES: N#Cc1ccc2cc1CCOCc1cc(c(F)cc1Cc1nc3c(OCCOC)cc(C(=O)OC)cc3n1C[C@@H]1CCO1)-c1cccc(n1)OC2"
